L_{\\rm m}\\approx{4\\over 3}\\alpha\\beta L_{0},\\hskip 28.452756pt\\tau_{\\rm m} \\approx\\left[-{4\\beta\\over 3}\\ln\\left({16\\alpha^{2}\\beta\\over 3}\\right) \\right]^{1/2}. 

The L_{\\rm m} increases with \\alpha.

From the above discussion we see that, for \\alpha^{2}\\beta\\ll 1, the peak luminosity goes up as \\alpha increases; for \\alpha^{2}\\beta\\gg 1, the peak luminosity goes down as \\alpha increases. Thus, the peak luminosity reaches its maximum roughly at \\alpha^{2}\\beta\\sim 1 (or t_{\\rm rad}\\sim\\beta^{1/2}t_{\\rm c}, which is comparable with t_{\\rm c} for sub-relativistic case), where we have the biggest peak luminosity L_{m}\\sim\\beta^{1/2}L_{0}.

